= Arbitrage =

== Overview ==
'''Arbitrage''' is a trading strategy that seeks to profit from inconsistent prices or economically equivalent payoffs across markets, instruments, or states while minimizing or hedging net exposure to the underlying price movement.

In idealized finance, a pure arbitrage produces a non-negative payoff in every state and a positive payoff in at least one state without net initial cost. Real-world arbitrage is usually less perfect because of execution risk, capital constraints, fees, latency, settlement, inventory, and model risk.

In automated market makers, arbitrageurs trade when pool-implied prices diverge from external markets. Their trades can move reserve ratios toward external prices while extracting value from the discrepancy.

Arbitrage is not a guarantee of instant price equality. Frictions and risk create a band within which discrepancies may persist.

== Boundaries ==
* arbitrage ≠ ordinary speculation
* arbitrage opportunity ≠ guaranteed executable profit
* arbitrage pressure ≠ perfect price equality

